rticipants 6,640,819 but did not include Mar 2026; therefore the June 12, 2026 PDF is the first fetched official print containing Mar 2026."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Fetched the recent official-source reference class from the March-inclusive PDF and older official monthly rows.","result":"Recent same-month and nearby totals were Mar 2025 6,850,836, Feb 2025 6,802,175, Apr 2025 6,877,215, Mar 2024 6,680,974, and Mar 2023 6,621,311."},{"kind":"text","text":"Base-rate/reference-class view: March participation often sits above February, with Mar 2025 exceeding Feb 2025 by 48,661 and Mar 2024 exceeding Feb 2024 by 35,336. The same-vintage FY 2026 pattern is consistent with that seasonal rebound, from 6,664,580 in February to 6,701,661 in March."},{"kind":"text","text":"Counter-consideration: if this needed an ex-ante forecast before the June 12 official vintage, the late-2025 downshift and February weakness would have argued for a wider interval around roughly the high-6.6-million to low-6.7-million range. Once the first official print is fetched, that uncertainty is dominated by avoiding later revisions and matching the printed whole-count precision."},{"kind":"math","text":"Point uses the official first-print total directly: 1,458,253 women + 1,419,036 infants + 3,824,372 children = 6,701,661 total participants. The 80% interval is set to 6,701,660 to 6,701,662, one participant around the printed whole-count total, to reflect only transcription/vintage-selection risk after the official fixed vintage is observed."},{"kind":"forecast","point":6701661,"ciLow":6701660,"ciHigh":6701662}]}
