enrollment data.","result":"CMS distinguishes 2 data vintages, preliminary and updated, and states the data.medicaid.gov monthly enrollment data are updated monthly."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Fetched the recent preliminary-vintage reference class from the official CMS data page for reporting periods 202511 through 202602.","result":"Official-source recent preliminary totals used were 78,620,000 for November 2025, 78,468,000 for December 2025, 78,312,000 for January 2026, and 78,184,000 for February 2026."},{"kind":"text","text":"Base rate: the comparable preliminary series was falling by about 128,000 to 156,000 per month across the latest three month-to-month moves, far below the steep unwinding losses of 2023-2024."},{"kind":"tool","tool":"official.lookup","call":"Checked the official monthly page for adjacent releases around the target month.","result":"The same official release list showed February 2026 preliminary and updated entries, January 2026 preliminary and updated entries, and the March 2026 preliminary entry, all with June 26, 2026 visible on the page."},{"kind":"text","text":"Level and momentum: starting from 78,184,000 in February, I use a judgmental inside-view adjustment that keeps the decline slightly milder than the recent -145,000 average because the latest move was the smallest of the three fetched changes at about -128,000."},{"kind":"text","text":"Policy and one-off mechanisms: upside would come from late state processing stabilization, continuous-eligibility effects, or stronger CHIP retention; downside would come from renewal backlogs, eligibility redeterminations, and state reporting corrections hitting the preliminary count."},{"kind":"math","text":"Recent monthly declines were roughly -152,000, -156,000, and -128,000, with an average near -145,000. I use a milder March decline of -124,000 from 78,184,000, giving 78,060,000. The 80% interval is about 5.6 times the recent average absolute one-month move to allow for 